  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 302 AM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nWarm and Humid with Daily Storm Chances:\n\nOverall, North and Central Georgia will remain in moist southwest \nflow through most of the long term period. The mid-level low parked \nover the TN Valley will finally slide north Thursday as it gets \nsqueezed between a ridge pushing in from the Western Atlantic, and \nbroad troughing across the Central US. Coverage of showers and \nstorms will be scattered to numerous through Friday, with plenty of \nmoisture and instability remaining over the region. While no \nwidespread severe weather is expected, a few strong storms will be \npossible during the period, with strong winds as the primary threat, \nin addition to localized flash flooding from the high moisture \ncontent. Model guidance remains in good agreement on this typical \nsummertime pattern persisting through the upcoming weekend into \nearly next week. High temperatures will gradually warm up to the low \nto mid 90s by this weekend as the ridge builds further over the \nregion. Heat index values will rise to the upper 90s to 100 across \nNorth GA, with low 100s across Central GA.",
